# Core Architecture Rules

## Boundary separation

- Keep API transport concerns in server/api packages.
- Keep lifecycle state transitions centralized in lifecycle domain code.
- Keep execution and file operations in dedicated services, not mixed into handlers.
- Keep provider-specific logic inside provider adapters only.

## Dependency direction

- HTTP handlers depend on service interfaces, not concrete provider implementations.
- Lifecycle, execution, and file services may depend on provider ports and metadata ports.
- Provider adapters must not import HTTP layer models or handler packages.

## Lifecycle authority

- Lifecycle transitions must be explicit and validated (`creating`, `running`, `stopped`, `pruned`, etc.).
- Do not add hidden transitions (for example implicit auto-resume) without ADR and contract updates.
- Polling/status reads must not mutate activity state by default.

## Capacity and reconciliation

- Capacity decisions and reclamation policy belong to capacity/lifecycle domains.
- Reconciliation logic should be reusable for startup and periodic correction paths.
- Timer/retry state must be persisted; do not rely on in-memory-only scheduling.

## Change checklist

- When adding a new package, define its boundary and owner responsibilities.
- When changing service interfaces, update architecture docs and dependent rules/docs.
